## Package comparison

The Phase 3 package remains aligned with the approved plan, and the cumulative diff remains within its allowed client/protocol/test paths. Active-client terminal errors, owned active listeners, EOF finalization, late-frame guards, and future `CONNECTION_LOST` semantics improve the implementation, but the package is not complete.

## Findings

### High — The claimed handshake-to-active reader ownership is still not implemented

The handshake still installs temporary `data`/`error`/`close` listeners, calls `cleanup()` before resolving, awaits that Promise, constructs `Client`, and only then calls `client.attach()`. This is the same detached-listener handoff called out in Round 1; the new owned callbacks cover only the active `Client`, not the socket lifetime. A frame delivered after handshake cleanup and before `attach()` has no reader.

Handshake also still has no `end` callback and never calls `framer.end()`, so partial EOF during hello is reported only later as generic close/loss rather than invalid daemon framing. The initial connect Promise leaves its `error` listener installed after successful connect as well. Replace these separate listener sets with the package's single handshaking/active/terminal state machine and exact lifetime callbacks; transition active before resolving readiness.

### High — Most required deterministic client evidence remains absent

No client tests were added in this round, so the Round 1 acceptance gap remains. The suite still has only the Phase 2 handshake cases plus one reverse-order response test. It does not prove:

- unsolicited and duplicate responses cannot settle pending or later requests;
- malformed/mismatched success makes pending work indeterminate and future work connection-lost;
- typed request-local failure leaves the connection usable;
- network error/end/close races settle once without replay or another connection;
- repeated disposal yields pending/future `DISPOSED` and ignores late callbacks;
- invalid seek emits no frame;
- state instance/revision authority and ordered notifications;
- throwing, self-unsubscribing, idempotently unsubscribed, and late subscribers;
- split/multiple frames, malformed nested frames, partial EOF, or gap-free handshake delivery.

The package requires an in-file scripted real-socket daemon seam using `NdjsonFramer`; the added ad hoc reverse-order server still uses a manual string parser and cannot drive the remaining scenarios.

### Medium — Listener snapshot delivery remains conditional on the live set

Publication snapshots the listener set but checks `set.has(listener)` before each callback. One listener can therefore remove another during the current delivery and suppress that callback, so current-frame delivery is not a stable iteration. Iterate the captured snapshot directly; idempotent unsubscribe should prevent future events, not mutate an already-started delivery.

